 * See LICENSE and COPYRIGHT for the license and GPL section 7 attribution terms.
 *
 * GAL Bridge (galbridge) - Global Address List for Roundcube - configuration
 *
 * Copy this file to <roundcube>/config/galbridge.inc.php (recommended) or to config.inc.php in this
 * directory and adjust the values - ONE of the two: Roundcube reads config/galbridge.inc.php only
 * when the plugin directory has no config.inc.php, and when both exist GAL Bridge disables every
 * book (log config_invalid, diag FAIL) instead of running on the file you did not edit; so does a
 * file that exists but cannot be loaded (not readable by the PHP user, no $config array).
 * config/galbridge.inc.php lies outside the plugin directory - which a web server must never serve
 * (README 2.6) - and survives a replacement of the plugin files; with Composer create it BEFORE
 * "composer require": Roundcube's plugin installer re-creates plugins/galbridge/config.inc.php at
 * every install and update with the owner and umask of the user running Composer (README 2.1). A
 * configuration in the plugin directory works as well; diag warns about it until diag --url has
 * checked that the web server does not hand it out.
 * NEVER put a real secret into this .dist file. Your configuration file holds the password of the
 * vmail database role: make it readable only by root and the PHP-FPM group, e.g. (iRedMail on
 * Linux: Roundcube lives in /opt/www/roundcubemail)
 *   cd /opt/www/roundcubemail
 *   install -o root -g www-data -m 0640 plugins/galbridge/config.inc.php.dist config/galbridge.inc.php
 *
 * Every option shown here has the same default built into the plugin (lib/GalBridge/Config.php),
 * so you only need to set what you want to change. Every galbridge_* option is administrator-only:
 * a stored user preference with such a name is ignored (never list galbridge_* options in
 * Roundcube's dont_override: SECURITY.md). A galbridge_* option or ROUNDCUBE_GALBRIDGE_* variable
 * that is not shown here - usually a typo, which would leave the option you meant at its default -
 * disables every book (log config_invalid, diag FAIL with the closest valid name). A numeric option
 * is a whole number (an integer, or a string of digits) within the range given next to it: any
 * other value is reported and the default is used; a value outside the range is reported and
 * clamped.
 *
 * KEEP EVERY galbridge_* OPTION IN THIS FILE (or in config/galbridge.inc.php), written as plain
 * assignments. With Roundcube's include_host_config the Host header of the request - which the
 * client chooses - selects an extra configuration file: a galbridge_* option set or changed there
 * (only the two book names may differ per host) disables every book for that host name (log
 * config_invalid naming the option; diag checks every such file). Do not pull options in with
 * include_once/require_once either: a second reading of the configuration in the same PHP process
 * does not see them (diag warns). Entries of domain lists may be Unicode (IDN); they are compared in their
 * lower-case ASCII form.
 *
 * TYPES MATTER. A switch is the PHP boolean true or false (the integers 1 and 0 are accepted as
 * well); the two "searchonly" options also take the string 'auto'. Anything else - above all a
 * string such as 'false', 'no' or 'off', which PHP would read as true - is NOT interpreted: it is
 * reported (log and diag: FAIL), EVERY book is disabled until it is corrected, and meanwhile the
 * switch itself is read as its restrictive value (off; hide_expired, mark_foreign_domains and
 * searchonly on). The same holds for galbridge_visibility_column (a string) and the two hidden lists
 * (galbridge_hidden_addresses, galbridge_hidden_localparts). A malformed domain deny-list
 * (galbridge_*_exclude) disables the book it protects, for every user; a malformed
 * galbridge_db_refused_users refuses every database connection. A malformed allow-list entry is
 * dropped (fewer domains, never more).
 *
 * Environment variables: Roundcube lets ROUNDCUBE_<OPTION NAME IN UPPER CASE> override any option
 * (typical in container deployments). For the switches of this plugin the variable must be exactly
 * 1, 0, true or false (searchonly options: also auto); "false" really means false here, although
 * Roundcube itself would turn that text into true. Any other spelling disables every book. diag
 * lists the overrides that are in effect (names only) and the effective value of every switch -
 * for the environment of the shell it runs in; the PHP of the web server (a PHP-FPM pool) has its
 * own, and the plugin logs the names of the variables in effect there once per session
 * (env_override).
 *
 * Enable the plugin by adding 'galbridge' to $config['plugins'] in Roundcube's
 * config/config.inc.php (see the last section), then check the installation from the Roundcube
 * directory, as the PHP user (exit code 0 = no FAIL, 2 = problems found or the run aborted; with
 * --strict a WARN line counts as a problem). diag refuses to run as root: it loads PHP code
 * from the plugin and the Roundcube directory (grants is the one command to run as root, with
 * --allow-root - see the VMAIL DATABASE section).
 *   sudo -u www-data plugins/galbridge/bin/galbridge.sh diag
 *   sudo -u www-data plugins/galbridge/bin/galbridge.sh diag --user=jan.kowalski@example.com
 *   sudo -u www-data plugins/galbridge/bin/galbridge.sh diag --url=https://mail.example.com/mail/
 *   sudo -u www-data plugins/galbridge/bin/galbridge.sh diag --strict
 * Without sudo (iRedMail does not install it), as root: runuser -u www-data -- plugins/galbridge/bin/galbridge.sh diag ...
 * With iRedMail's self-signed certificate: add --cafile=/etc/ssl/certs/iRedMail.crt to --url, and
 * use the host name of the certificate in the URL (not 127.0.0.1 or localhost).
 */

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// VMAIL DATABASE (required)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DSN of the iRedMail vmail database, read with a DEDICATED READ-ONLY role that has column-level
// SELECT grants only - never mailbox.password, recovery_email, allow_nets or settings - and that
// reads the forwardings through the view galbridge_forwardings (alias and list rows only), never
// the table itself. The server builds the rows of that view before it applies any condition of a
// query (MariaDB/MySQL ALGORITHM = TEMPTABLE with a LIMIT fence, PostgreSQL security_barrier with an
// OFFSET 0 fence), so even a query sent with the role's password never sees a private forwarding
// target (README 2.2). The plugin refuses a role that can do more than read the directory
// (role_too_broad): a secret column of any table, the forwardings table, a membership in another
// role, a role attribute, the ownership of the database, a global privilege, a privilege outside the
// directory or one to change anything - and a view that holds rows of another kind.
// '' = not configured: no book is offered (fail closed) and diag reports FAIL. There is no fallback
// to Roundcube's own db_dsnw: PostgreSQL cannot query across databases, and iRedMail's "roundcube"
// role (used by the password plugin) can UPDATE vmail.mailbox and read every password hash.
//
// Create the role from the reference grant set. Either print it, as ROOT from the Roundcube
// directory, with a fresh random password - the SQL on standard output, the matching DSN line on
// standard error (the terminal; it is printed as an SQL comment -
// paste it here WITHOUT the leading "-- ", then check this file with "php -l"), nothing written to disk:
//   umask 077; plugins/galbridge/bin/galbridge.sh grants --allow-root --driver=mysql [--host=localhost]
//       [--port=N] [--account-host=localhost] [--db=vmail] [--role=galbridge] [--connection-limit=25] > /root/galbridge-grants.sql
//   umask 077; plugins/galbridge/bin/galbridge.sh grants --allow-root --driver=pgsql [--host=127.0.0.1]
//       [--port=N] [--db=vmail] [--role=galbridge] [--connection-limit=25] > /root/galbridge-grants.sql
// (--host = the database server written into the DSN, --port = its port when it is not the default
// one - MariaDB/MySQL with --host=127.0.0.1, as localhost is the unix socket; --account-host = MariaDB/MySQL host part of
// the account, i.e. where Roundcube connects from - refused for PostgreSQL, whose pg_hba.conf limits
// the addresses; README section 2.2). The database administrator runs that output, so it must not
// come from code or a process of the web server user: --allow-root refuses code that root does not
// own alone, and grants refuses to run as a user that can read this file (the web server user) -
// check the plugin files with find / namei first, review the output and apply it with
// "mysql --binary-mode <" or "runuser -u postgres -- psql -X -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d vmail <"
// (README 2.2). Or take SQL/grants.mysql.sql / SQL/grants.postgres.sql from the verified release
// archive, copy it where only root can read it, edit the copy and run it the same way. The output
// has three forms:
//   (default)         CREATES the role. When a role of that name already exists it stops and
//                     changes nothing - a typo cannot take over the role of another component;
//   --reset-password  gives the (new or existing) role a NEW password: the old one stops working;
//   --grants-only     re-applies attributes, limits, view and grants to the existing role; its
//                     password and the DSN stay as they are (use it after an upgrade of GAL Bridge,
//                     README section 5).
// Names of roles that belong to iRedMail, Roundcube or the database server are refused. The
// personal-data columns (telephone, mobile, birthday) are a commented-out grant: uncomment it only
// for the fields you enable in galbridge_fields. Keep the output private. The statements never
// contain the password (one exception: --clear-text-password, below): they carry a VERIFIER of it
// (PostgreSQL: SCRAM-SHA-256; MariaDB/MySQL: the mysql_native_password hash), so no log of the
// database server - statement log, slow query log, binary log, their replicas and backups - can
// record it. The clear text is in the DSN line on standard error only. Treat the verifier as a
// secret as well: together with a recorded login handshake of the role - possible only on a link
// without TLS - it allows a login (SECURITY.md, "Logs"; after an exposure: --reset-password).
// (MySQL 8.4 and later, which no longer load mysql_native_password: add --clear-text-password;
// MySQL rewrites passwords in its own logs. With it the SQL output itself holds the password in
// clear text - its header says so: keep that file private and delete it after use. That output
// stops on MariaDB, which does not rewrite them, before the statement with the password - also
// through a client that drops comments.)
//
// Checked before anything connects (a violation disables every book; diag and the log say why):
//  - driver mysql (iRedMail writes "mysqli", accepted as well) or pgsql;
//  - the DSN names its target itself: a host or a unix socket, a port of digits only, a database
//    name, and only the parameters shown in the examples below (plus charset and cipher for
//    MariaDB/MySQL); a parameter that is unknown, repeated or malformed is refused, and so is the
//    PostgreSQL 'service' parameter. Environment variables of the PostgreSQL client library that
//    would move the connection elsewhere (PGHOSTADDR, PGSERVICE, PGSERVICEFILE, or PGPORT for a DSN
//    without a port) are refused as well;
//  - the DSN is written in the form of the examples: scheme://user:password@TARGET/database?params
//    with TARGET one of host, host:port, [IPv6], [IPv6]:port or unix(/path/to/socket) (PostgreSQL:
//    the socket directory, unix(/var/run/postgresql:5433) for another port), the database name as
//    it is (no percent-encoding) and a '+' in a parameter value written as %2B. Anything Roundcube's
//    DSN parser would drop or read differently (a second port, text around an IPv6 address, the
//    unix+ / tcp() notations, a '?' before the database) is refused; the DSN handed to the driver is
//    rebuilt from the checked parts. A unix socket path has at most 107 bytes (PostgreSQL: the
//    directory plus "/.s.PGSQL.<port>");
//  - the role must not be one of the refused roles (galbridge_db_refused_users below);
//  - local (a unix socket, 127.0.0.0/8, ::1; MariaDB/MySQL "localhost", which is a unix socket;
//    PostgreSQL "localhost" with a warning, because libpq looks the name up through the system
//    resolver on every connection - write 127.0.0.1) may be plaintext; ANY other host needs
//    VERIFIED TLS - PostgreSQL sslmode=verify-full (sslmode=require encrypts but does not
//    authenticate the server and is refused; verify-ca does not check the host name and needs
//    galbridge_db_allow_verify_ca), MySQL/MariaDB ca= or capath= together with verify_server_cert=1
//    - unless galbridge_db_allow_plaintext is set.
// After connecting, a role that can read mailbox.password, recovery_email or allow_nets, or the
// forwardings TABLE, is refused whatever its name (log reason "role_too_broad"): a broad role is
// never "good enough".
// URL-encode (rawurlencode) a password that contains characters other than A-Z a-z 0-9 - . _ ~
//
// MariaDB / MySQL (iRedMail MySQL edition):
//   TCP on the same host (as iRedMail configures Roundcube itself):
//     'mysql://galbridge:PASSWORD@127.0.0.1/vmail'
//   unix socket (Debian/Ubuntu path; RHEL family: /var/lib/mysql/mysql.sock):
//     'mysql://galbridge:PASSWORD@unix(/run/mysqld/mysqld.sock)/vmail'
//   'localhost' as the host IS a unix socket: the one PHP's pdo_mysql.default_socket names (diag
//   shows it on its DSN line), never TCP - so it takes no port (localhost:3307 is refused: write
//   127.0.0.1:3307), and neither does unix(...). The host part of the
//   account must match the way you connect: use the one of iRedMail's own roundcube account
//   (SHOW GRANTS FOR 'roundcube'@'localhost';) and pass it to "grants --account-host=...".
//   Remote server with verified TLS (the DSN host name must match the server certificate; the
//   account needs REQUIRE SSL, which "grants" adds for a non-local --account-host):
//     'mysql://galbridge:PASSWORD@db.example.com/vmail?ca=/etc/ssl/certs/vmail-db-ca.pem&verify_server_cert=1'
//     'mysql://galbridge:PASSWORD@db.example.com/vmail?capath=/etc/ssl/certs&verify_server_cert=1'
//   With a client certificate in addition (REQUIRE X509), append
//     &cert=/etc/roundcube/galbridge-client.pem&key=/etc/roundcube/galbridge-client.key
//
// PostgreSQL (iRedMail PostgreSQL edition; its pg_hba.conf accepts md5 password logins over the
// local socket - "local all all md5" - and, through the catch-all line "host all all 0.0.0.0/0 md5",
// over TCP from any IPv4 address that listen_addresses lets in, for EVERY database: insert the four
// lines of README 2.2 - "local all galbridge reject" and the three host lines - BEFORE iRedMail's
// "local all all md5" line):
//   TCP on the same host (as iRedMail configures Roundcube itself; the lines of README 2.2):
//     'pgsql://galbridge:PASSWORD@127.0.0.1/vmail'
//   unix socket (give the socket DIRECTORY; pg_hba.conf then needs
//   "local vmail galbridge scram-sha-256" on the line before "local all galbridge reject"):
//     'pgsql://galbridge:PASSWORD@unix(/var/run/postgresql)/vmail'
//   Remote server with verified TLS (pg_hba.conf needs "hostssl vmail galbridge 192.0.2.10/32
//   scram-sha-256" and "hostnossl vmail galbridge 0.0.0.0/0 reject" inserted BEFORE iRedMail's
//   catch-all line - pg_hba.conf uses the first matching line, a line after it never applies; give
//   sslrootcert explicitly, the PHP user normally has no ~/.postgresql/root.crt):
//     'pgsql://galbridge:PASSWORD@db.example.com/vmail?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=/etc/ssl/certs/vmail-db-ca.pem'
//   With a client certificate in addition, append
//     &sslcert=/etc/roundcube/galbridge-client.crt&sslkey=/etc/roundcube/galbridge-client.key
//   Optional parameter: application_name=galbridge (shown in pg_stat_activity).
$config['galbridge_db_dsn'] = '';

// Connect timeout in seconds (1 .. 30), added to the DSN as "timeout" unless the DSN already has
// one. Without it the drivers' built-in default lets a single connection attempt to an unreachable
// server take 30 seconds (pdo_pgsql and PDO MySQL alike), and every webmail request that touches
// an address book (opening a message, compose, autocomplete) would hang that long. It bounds the
// TCP connection. Together with galbridge_db_timeout below it also
// sets the READ timeout of the MariaDB/MySQL connection - the larger of the two plus 2 s, 5 s by
// default - as mysqlnd.net_read_timeout while the plugin's own connection is created (restored right
// after; a lower php.ini value is kept): without it, a server or proxy that accepted the connection
// and then stays silent would hold the PHP worker for mysqlnd's default of 86400 s. PostgreSQL has no
// read timeout after connecting: set PHP-FPM's request_terminate_timeout (SECURITY.md).
// After a failed connection of an unreachable or slow server (the attempt took at least half of this
// timeout, or the error says the server cannot be reached) - or a connection lost during a
// statement - the plugin does not try again for 30 seconds in that session (log reason "db_down").
// A refusal the server answers at once (its connection limit: "db_busy"; an authentication error or
// a limit text in the server's language: "db_down cause=refused") fails only that request. Meanwhile a session keeps listing
// the books it already had, and opening or searching them shows "The directory is temporarily
// unavailable."; a session that has not resolved its books yet (e.g. a new login) gets none until
// the database is back. An outage is never remembered as "no entitlement".
$config['galbridge_db_connect_timeout'] = 2;

// Statement timeout in seconds (1 .. 30), set for the plugin's database session right after
// connecting, together with read-only transaction mode: MariaDB max_statement_time, MySQL
// max_execution_time, PostgreSQL statement_timeout. It bounds what one slow or abusive search can
// cost the database. This value - not the role - bounds the plugin's statements: the reference
// grants give the role 25 connections on BOTH engines (a hard limit; "grants --connection-limit=N",
// size it to at most the PHP-FPM pm.max_children of Roundcube AND what the database server can
// spare - iRedMail: keep 25, its MariaDB keeps max_connections = 151 and its PostgreSQL 100 on
// Debian/Ubuntu for every service, README 2.2) and a statement time of 5 seconds as its
// DEFAULT (PostgreSQL and MariaDB; MySQL 8 has none), which every session - this one too - may
// change for itself. A server that refuses this statement timeout or the read-only mode is not
// used: the books are unavailable (log reasons "session_limit_refused" and "db_down", diag FAIL
// naming the statement) - the plugin never reads without its statement bound. diag shows the
// statement time of the plugin's session (FAIL when it is not this value) and the limit of the
// account (WARN when it is unlimited).
$config['galbridge_db_timeout'] = 3;

// true: accept a NON-loopback database host without verified TLS (plaintext, or TLS without
// certificate verification such as sslmode=require). The role password and the directory data
// (names, addresses, lists) then cross the network unprotected, and whoever can intercept or
// redirect the connection (ARP or DNS spoofing) can read them or feed the plugin a forged
// directory. Only for a trusted, isolated network path (e.g. a WireGuard tunnel or a dedicated
// storage VLAN) - prefer TLS. While it is in effect, diag and the log warn; diag warns as long as it
// is true, also while the DSN names a local host (it would apply the moment the DSN changes).
$config['galbridge_db_allow_plaintext'] = false;

// true: accept PostgreSQL sslmode=verify-ca for a NON-loopback host. verify-ca checks that the
// server certificate comes from the CA in sslrootcert but NOT that it was issued for the host you
// connect to: anybody who holds a certificate of that CA can pose as the database server. That is
// acceptable only with a private CA that signs nothing but this database server. Prefer
// sslmode=verify-full. While it is in effect, diag and the log warn; diag warns as long as it is
// true, also while no DSN uses verify-ca.
$config['galbridge_db_allow_verify_ca'] = false;

// Database roles never accepted in galbridge_db_dsn (compared case-insensitively), because
// iRedMail gives them far more than GAL Bridge needs: vmail (Postfix, Dovecot, iRedAPD - reads
// every password hash), vmailadmin (iRedAdmin, mlmmjadmin - read-write), roundcube (the password
// plugin - UPDATE on vmail.mailbox), sogo, iredadmin, iredapd, amavisd, fail2ban (other services)
// and the superusers root and postgres. The users of Roundcube's db_dsnw and db_dsnr are refused in
// addition, as is the user of the password plugin's password_db_dsn - read from Roundcube's main
// configuration, or else from the password plugin's own file (plugins/password/config.inc.php, or
// config/password.inc.php when the plugin directory has none). A password plugin with its sql
// driver whose DSN is in none of these places is reported by diag (add its user here).
// The names below are ALWAYS refused: this option ADDS names (a list without them still refuses
// them), it cannot remove one. A value that is not a list, or a list with an entry that is not a
// role name (a number, an empty text, a nested list), refuses EVERY database connection (log
// config_invalid, diag FAIL naming the position of the entry) - a safety net is never guessed.
// diag shows the refused roles (INFO "refused roles"). "grants" never prints statements for one of
// the built-in names either.
$config['galbridge_db_refused_users'] = ['vmail', 'vmailadmin', 'roundcube', 'sogo', 'iredadmin', 'iredapd', 'amavisd', 'fail2ban', 'root', 'postgres'];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHO IS WHO (IMAP host binding and the viewer cache)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What a user sees is derived from the Roundcube login alone: it is looked up in vmail.mailbox
// (active mailbox in an active, non-backup-MX domain) and the home domain is that row's domain
// column - which has to be the domain of the login itself; a row where the two differ opens no
// directory. That is safe only when the login was authenticated by an IMAP server that uses THIS
// vmail database. A Roundcube where the IMAP server can be chosen at login (a host field, an
// imap_host array, or a template with %-placeholders such as %d/%n/%s, which come from the
// client's Host header or the login) would let anybody log in through an IMAP server of their own
// as ceo@tenant-a.example and receive tenant A's directory.
//
// Allowed IMAP servers: host names or IP addresses, optionally with a port - NO scheme and no
// placeholder - e.g. ['127.0.0.1'], ['mail.example.com:993', 'imap2.example.com:993'] or
// ['[2001:db8::10]:993']. An entry without a port allows every port of that host (diag warns for
// each such entry); give the port whenever another IMAP service (a test instance, a proxy to
// another user database) listens on the same host. The host stored for a Roundcube user
// (users.mail_host) has no port and is compared by host. Both the IMAP server of the session (host and port) and the host stored for the
// Roundcube user must be covered, compared exactly as Roundcube wrote them at login (a host name,
// an IP address - IPv6 in brackets - or a socket; anything else, e.g. a typed server with a blank
// or a colon, is host_mismatch: PHP would have connected it somewhere else than it reads).
// The one scheme that is accepted is a local socket in Roundcube's own notation,
// ['unix:///run/dovecot/imap.sock'] (an absolute path of printable ASCII, without blanks and
// without %): it has neither host name nor port and is matched as a whole, the path exactly as
// written. A path with another character is refused here (the plugin is disabled), and a session
// or stored host with one is host_mismatch.
// [] = derive it from Roundcube's imap_host when that is ONE host without placeholders, together
// with its port - or one local socket. iRedMail writes 'tls://127.0.0.1:143', which gives
// '127.0.0.1:143': nothing to set. With an array or a templated imap_host and no explicit list the
// plugin stays disabled (diag: FAIL), and a malformed entry disables it as well (fail closed). The
// same holds when Roundcube's
// include_host_config is enabled (or the variable ROUNDCUBE_INCLUDE_HOST_CONFIG is set, which
// Roundcube ignores for it): imap_host can then differ per requested host name (the Host header
// selects the configuration file), so nothing is derived from it - list the servers here (in this
// file: a list in a per-host file disables every book for that host name). Nothing is derived
// either when imap_host as Roundcube applies it differs from a second reading of the configuration
// files (an environment-specific config-<env>.inc.php, a file pulled in with include_once, another
// plugin): diag names the options that differ.
// EVERY listed server must authenticate against this vmail database; the plugin cannot verify that
// and diag reminds you when more than one is listed. diag checks that the list covers imap_host
// and the hosts stored for the existing Roundcube users; a session outside it is logged as
// host_mismatch with the session server, the stored host and the allowed servers.
$config['galbridge_imap_hosts'] = [];

// The directory trusts the IMAP login of a session: whoever can answer that login can claim any
// mailbox. A directory IMAP server that is NOT local (anything but a unix:/// socket, localhost,
// 127.0.0.0/8 or ::1) must therefore be reached over TLS that authenticates the server:
// imap_host 'ssl://host:993' (TLS from the first byte), or 'tls://host:143' (STARTTLS) on
// Roundcube 1.7.7 or later - older releases, and a core that reports '1.7-git', go on in plain
// text when the server does not offer STARTTLS - with PHP's openssl extension and without
// imap_conn_options that switch certificate verification off (verify_peer, verify_peer_name,
// allow_self_signed). A session on such a server without it gets no directory (log
// imap_transport, diag FAIL). true accepts it anyway (log and diag: WARN) - only for a link nobody
// else can reach. iRedMail's default 'tls://127.0.0.1:143' is local: nothing to do.
$config['galbridge_imap_allow_plaintext'] = false;

// Seconds (30 .. 3600) for which the resolved viewer - home domain, the viewer's own alias
// addresses, the column/privilege probe and therefore the book entitlements - is cached in the
// Roundcube session. Losing the DIRECTORY does not wait for it: every request that reads the
// directory first confirms, with one small statement, that the mailbox of the session is still
// active, unexpired and in its domain - a mailbox that was disabled, expired, deleted or moved (or
// whose domain was) loses the directory with its next request; with the strict list policy the
// viewer's own aliases are read again in the same step, so a REMOVED alias applies with the next
// request as well (that request reads nothing), and a NEW one from the request after it (the request
// that reads it keeps the old, narrower set). When the cached entry is renewed after this time,
// the IMAP server confirms the credentials of the session first (Roundcube's own connection): a
// changed password or a disabled IMAP service ends the directory for the rest of that session.
// A changed column grant and the role checks apply after at most this time. A
// change of THIS configuration takes effect immediately as well (the cache is bound to a keyed
// fingerprint of it). Lower = fresher, one lookup (and one IMAP login in an address-book request)
// more per period and session.
$config['galbridge_scope_ttl'] = 300;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DOMAIN ADDRESS BOOK (source id "galbridge_domain")
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The everyday directory: the people, alias accounts and mailing lists of the viewer's own domain
// (or of the viewer's organization, see galbridge_organizations). It has the same per-domain scope
// as iRedMail's LDAP edition (base_dn domainName=%d), so it is safe on a multi-tenant server: no
// tenant ever sees another one through this book.
$config['galbridge_domain_book'] = true;

// Name in the list of address books. '' = the organization label when the viewer's domain belongs
// to one of galbridge_organizations, otherwise the localised "Domain directory".
$config['galbridge_domain_book_name'] = '';

// Viewer domains that get the domain book: ['*'] = every hosted domain (default; '*' is honoured
// only as the sole element), or a list such as ['example.com', 'example.net']. This is an
// allow-list: an entry that cannot be normalised is dropped and reported (ERROR in the log, WARN in
// diag).
$config['galbridge_domain_viewer_domains'] = ['*'];

// Viewer domains that never get the domain book (wins over the list above), e.g. a customer who
// has not agreed to a directory. This is a deny-list: when it is malformed (not an array, an entry
// that cannot be normalised, or '*') the domain book is disabled for EVERYBODY (fail closed, diag
// FAIL) instead of silently showing it to the domains you meant to exclude.
$config['galbridge_domain_viewer_domains_exclude'] = [];

// Groups of the domain book:
//  'auto'       - 'domain' when the viewer's domain belongs to an organization of more than one
//                 domain, else 'department'
//  'department' - one group per value of mailbox.department, compared case-insensitively (needs
//                 'department' in galbridge_fields and its column granted; otherwise the book has
//                 no groups and the log says why). In an organization of several domains equal
//                 department names of its domains form one group (one trust unit).
//  'domain'     - one group per domain of the organization
//  'none'       - no groups
// Groups contain persons only (alias accounts and lists stay single entries), so member counts
// are head counts. Expanding a group (compose to group, a group picked in autocomplete, group
// export) yields all members or - above galbridge_max_group_members - nothing and a visible
// warning (a refused group export downloads no file; a download cannot show a message), never a
// silently truncated recipient list. A group id is honoured only for a group the book shows: a
// book without groups (grouping 'none', search-only, or more groups than galbridge_max_groups)
// selects nothing by such an id. iRedMail: the open-source iRedAdmin does not
// fill mailbox.department; without values there are simply no department groups.
$config['galbridge_domain_groups'] = 'auto';

// Search-only book: true = listing it without a search shows nothing but Roundcube's "search only"
// notice (as for LDAP directories); entries appear through search and autocomplete only - a
// search-only book has no groups. false (default) = browsable like a personal address book.
// 'auto' = search-only (and without groups) when the book holds more than
// galbridge_searchonly_threshold entries.
$config['galbridge_domain_searchonly'] = false;

// Offer the book in compose autocomplete. The plugin appends the source to Roundcube's
// autocomplete_addressbooks itself (for compose, Redirect-dialog and autocomplete requests only).
// Enforced on the server: with false, an autocomplete request that names this source gets no
// contacts and no groups.
$config['galbridge_domain_autocomplete'] = true;

// Allow exporting the WHOLE book as vCard (Export without a selection or search), up to
// galbridge_max_export entries; false refuses it. A refused export downloads nothing (the server
// answers 204 No Content and logs the reason; an export is a file download and cannot show a
// message). Exporting selected contacts, a search result or a group stays possible within their own
// limits (it shows nothing the user cannot already see on screen). The whole of a SEARCH-ONLY book
// (galbridge_domain_searchonly = true, or 'auto' above galbridge_searchonly_threshold) is refused
// whatever this option says.
// EVERY export is complete or refused, never cut: a search result with more matches than the
// result cap (galbridge_max_results), a set of which an entry could not be read, or a directory
// that fails during the export all answer 204 instead of a file that silently lacks entries.
$config['galbridge_domain_export'] = true;

// Organizations: several domains of ONE customer that share one domain book, e.g.
//   $config['galbridge_organizations'] = [
//       'Acme' => ['acme.example', 'acme-de.example', 'acme-uk.example'],
//       'Example Ltd' => ['example-ltd.example', 'example-ltd-ie.example'],
//   ];
// A viewer whose domain is listed sees all domains of its organization; the key is the label used
// as the book name (unless galbridge_domain_book_name is set) and as the 'organization' field.
// Domains that are not listed see only themselves. The sets must be disjoint: a domain listed in
// two organizations is removed from both (it falls back to its own domain - fail closed) and diag
// reports FAIL. List primary (mailbox) domains; iRedMail alias domains never hold mailboxes.
// SECURITY: an organization merges directories - never put two different customers into one.
$config['galbridge_organizations'] = [];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// GLOBAL ADDRESS BOOK (source id "galbridge_global"; cross-domain, OFF by default)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One additional book that shows entries of OTHER domains to selected viewer domains, e.g. for a
// group of companies hosted on one server. It is deliberately stricter than the domain book:
// prefix search only (whatever addressbook_search_mode says); e-mail addresses are matched on the
// local part (or exactly in strict mode), never on a domain fragment, and the 'organization' field
// only by its complete label, so searching for a domain or organization name cannot list a whole
// tenant; EVERY search word must reach galbridge_min_search_length; and a lower result cap
// (galbridge_global_max_results). On a server that hosts unrelated customers
// think twice: this book discloses one customer's people to another.
$config['galbridge_global_book'] = false;

// Name in the list of address books. '' = the localised "Global directory".
$config['galbridge_global_book_name'] = '';

// Viewer domains that get the global book. [] (default) = nobody, so switching the book on alone
// shows nothing; ['*'] = every hosted domain. Allow-list: invalid entries are dropped (ERROR in the
// log, WARN in diag).
$config['galbridge_global_viewer_domains'] = [];

// Viewer domains that never get the global book (deny-list, wins over the list above; malformed =
// the global book is disabled for everybody).
$config['galbridge_global_viewer_domains_exclude'] = [];

// Domains whose entries the global book shows, e.g. ['acme.example', 'partner.example']. ['*'] = every
// hosted domain, INCLUDING tenants added later - honoured only together with
// galbridge_global_allow_wildcard = true (otherwise the book stays empty and diag warns).
$config['galbridge_global_data_domains'] = [];

// Domains never shown in the global book, also when galbridge_global_data_domains is ['*']
// (deny-list; malformed = the global book is disabled).
$config['galbridge_global_data_domains_exclude'] = [];

// Second, explicit switch for '*' in galbridge_global_data_domains; diag warns while it is in
// effect, and while it is true without '*' (a later '*' would then list every domain at once).
// Never enable it on a server that hosts unrelated customers.
$config['galbridge_global_allow_wildcard'] = false;

// false (default): the viewer's own domain (or organization) is left out of the global book - it is
// already in the domain book, and every colleague would otherwise appear twice in autocomplete.
// true: show it in the global book as well (e.g. when the domain book is switched off).
$config['galbridge_global_include_home'] = false;

// Groups of the global book: 'none' (default), 'domain' (one group per domain shown) or
// 'department' (needs the department field): one group per DOMAIN and department, labelled
// "(domain) Department" - equal department names of different tenants never form one group, so no
// tenant can add its accounts to another tenant's group. The number of such groups depends on the
// data of every tenant in the book; above galbridge_max_groups the book shows no groups at all
// (fail closed; diag WARN) - prefer 'domain'. Group expansion follows the same all-or-nothing rule
// as in the domain book.
$config['galbridge_global_groups'] = 'none';

// Search-only (see galbridge_domain_searchonly). Default true: a cross-domain directory is meant
// to be searched, not browsed.
$config['galbridge_global_searchonly'] = true;

// Offer the global book in compose autocomplete (enforced on the server, as for the domain book).
// Roundcube sorts the suggestions of all address books by name and pre-selects the first one; the
// entries of other domains start with their "(domain)" marker (galbridge_mark_foreign_domains).
// From Roundcube 1.7.6 it sorts with the ICU collator, where '(' comes before letters and digits -
// so they are listed BEFORE unmarked names. Roundcube 1.7.0 - 1.7.5 sorts with the C library locale
// of the user's language, which usually ignores '(': they are listed among the unmarked names by
// the domain in their marker (first only in the C locale). With autocomplete_threads the browser
// lists the answer of each book in the order it arrives. diag describes the order of your core.
// Set false where that order is not wanted: the book then stays out of autocomplete and remains
// available in the address book and in the contact list of the compose window.
$config['galbridge_global_autocomplete'] = true;

// Allow exporting the WHOLE global book as vCard. Default false: a bulk export of other domains'
// people is refused; selected contacts, search results and groups stay exportable. A search-only
// global book (galbridge_global_searchonly = true, the default, or 'auto' above the threshold) is
// never exported as a whole, whatever this option says.
$config['galbridge_global_export'] = false;

// Entries (persons + alias accounts + lists) above which a book with searchonly = 'auto' becomes
// search-only. Applies to both books.
$config['galbridge_searchonly_threshold'] = 2000;   // 1 .. 1000000

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CONTENT (fields, and which entries are listed)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Field allowlist (data minimisation). A field that is not listed is never shown, searched, sorted
// on, grouped on or exported. Available fields:
//   'name'          display name: mailbox.name, else first + last name, else the local part (always on)
//   'email'         the mailbox address, plus alias addresses with galbridge_user_aliases (always on)
//   'firstname'     mailbox.first_name           'surname'   mailbox.last_name
//   'department'    mailbox.department
//   'phone'         mailbox.telephone (work)     'mobile'    mailbox.mobile
//   'organization'  the label from galbridge_organizations (not read from vmail; in the global book
//                   a search word matches it only when it equals the whole label)
//   'birthday'      mailbox.birthday (never searchable)
// The effective set is this list minus the columns the database role cannot read: grant the
// optional columns for exactly the fields you enable (SQL/grants.*.sql grant department,
// first_name and last_name; telephone, mobile and birthday are a commented-out statement). A field
// whose column is missing or not granted is dropped with a warning instead of breaking the book,
// and diag lists granted columns that no configured field uses (INFO). iRedMail:
// first_name, last_name, telephone, mobile and birthday exist since iRedMail 1.7.3, and the
// open-source iRedAdmin fills only mailbox.name - so set addressbook_sort_col = 'name' (last section).
// PRIVACY: phone, mobile and birthday are personal data shown to every viewer of the book; enable
// them only with a legal basis (e.g. GDPR art. 6) and an internal policy.
// The value must be an array: anything else (a string of names, an environment variable that is
// not JSON) shows name and e-mail only and is reported (log, diag: WARN).
$config['galbridge_fields'] = ['name', 'firstname', 'surname', 'email', 'department'];

// Per-user alias addresses (iRedMail forwardings rows with is_alias = 1, e.g. jan@example.com for
// jan.kowalski@example.com): true = shown as an additional ("other") address of their owner and
// matched by searches. Default false: they are neither shown nor searchable, so a search for an
// alias cannot reveal who receives it (e.g. which person is behind ceo@ or abuse@).
// With true, showing and matching follow ONE rule: an alias counts only when its domain belongs to
// the book and is active (not a backup MX, not expired with galbridge_hide_expired), when it follows
// the address rule of the directory (README 3.4) and when it is on neither hidden list below - a
// hidden alias is not shown and does not find its owner, whole or by a fragment. At most 20 aliases
// are shown per person (the first in address order) - for every person of a page. They are read
// with a window function: MariaDB 10.2 / MySQL 8.0 or later (diag FAILs on an older server).
// The viewer's OWN aliases (up to 200, of deliverable domains) are always used internally to decide
// which restricted lists the viewer may see; with more than that the login alone counts.
$config['galbridge_user_aliases'] = false;

// iRedMail alias accounts (table "alias": an address that forwards to its members, "Mail alias"
// in iRedAdmin) are listed as single entries, subject to galbridge_list_policy.
$config['galbridge_include_aliases'] = true;

// mlmmj mailing lists (table "maillists") are listed as single entries, subject to
// galbridge_list_policy. Their subscribers are kept by mlmmj on disk, not in SQL, and never shown.
$config['galbridge_include_mailing_lists'] = true;

// Who sees alias accounts and mailing lists, following their iRedAPD posting policy (the
// accesspolicy column):
//  'strict'      - only viewers who may post to them (default): public or empty = everybody in the
//                  book; domain = viewers of the entry's domain; subdomain = viewers of that
//                  domain and its sub-domains; membersonly, moderatorsonly and
//                  membersandmoderatorsonly = members, moderators or owners among the viewer's
//                  addresses (mlmmj lists: moderators and owners only, subscribers are not in SQL).
//                  An unknown policy hides the entry (fail closed).
//  'public_only' - only entries with a public (or empty) policy
//  'all'         - every active entry whatever its policy. This reveals restricted lists (e.g.
//                  board@ or hr-cases@) to people who cannot even write to them: explicit opt-in.
$config['galbridge_list_policy'] = 'strict';

// Hide mailboxes, alias accounts, lists and domains whose iRedMail expiry time (the "expired"
// column; 9999-12-31 means never) has passed - by the database server's clock in its session time
// zone AND by UTC, whichever comes first: the column has no time zone, and an entry is hidden from
// its expiry on whichever of the two conventions wrote it. false = ignore
// expiry (the expired columns are then not needed). With true the expiry has to be READABLE: when
// the role cannot read the expired column of alias accounts or of lists, that kind of entry is left
// out of the books (diag: WARN) - it is never listed unfiltered.
$config['galbridge_hide_expired'] = true;

// Addresses that are never listed or matched in any book - persons, alias accounts, lists and
// per-user aliases (galbridge_user_aliases) - e.g. ['ceo@example.com', 'backup-robot@example.com'].
// Deny-list: a malformed list disables ALL books (fail closed, diag FAIL). The lists act on the
// entries the books offer: only addresses that follow the address rule (lower-case ASCII letters,
// digits and . _ + = - in the local part, an ASCII host name; README 3.4) are offered at all, so an
// entry written in another spelling cannot escape them (diag lists the entries not offered).
$config['galbridge_hidden_addresses'] = [];

// Local parts hidden in EVERY domain, e.g. ['postmaster', 'abuse', 'root', 'noreply'] (iRedMail
// creates postmaster@ of the first domain as a real mailbox); they hide per-user aliases with such
// a local part as well. Deny-list: malformed = all books disabled.
$config['galbridge_hidden_localparts'] = [];

// Name of an optional, administrator-created column of vmail.mailbox that decides per person
// whether they are listed (1 = listed, any other value = hidden). '' = no such column (default).
// The column is not part of the iRedMail schema and iRedAdmin does not know it: create it with a
// default, so that mailboxes added later get a defined value, and grant it to the role, e.g.
//   MariaDB:    ALTER TABLE vmail.mailbox ADD COLUMN galbridge_visible TINYINT(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 1;
//               GRANT SELECT (galbridge_visible) ON vmail.mailbox TO 'galbridge'@'localhost';
//   PostgreSQL: ALTER TABLE mailbox ADD COLUMN galbridge_visible INT2 NOT NULL DEFAULT 1;
//               GRANT SELECT (galbridge_visible) ON mailbox TO galbridge;
// (an integer column, not a PostgreSQL BOOLEAN). A configured column that the role cannot read
// lists no person at all (fail closed); an invalid name (lower-case letters, digits and '_', not
// starting with a digit, at most 64 characters) disables all books. The value must be a STRING:
// '' is the only way to say "no column" - an array, a number or a boolean (false included) is a
// configuration error that disables all books, never "no filter". diag shows the column in use.
// It applies to persons only; hide alias accounts and lists with galbridge_hidden_addresses.
$config['galbridge_visibility_column'] = '';

// In the global book, entries from outside the viewer's own domain (or organization) are shown as
// "(domain) Name", so that a look-alike name in another tenant is not mistaken for a colleague.
// The marker comes FIRST: recipient fields shorten a long name at its end, which must not hide it.
// Such entries carry no first and last name, so every "List names as" mode of Roundcube
// (addressbook_name_listing) shows the marked name. The marker is part of the display name: it also
// appears in recipient fields, exported vCards and contacts copied to a personal address book.
// Keep {name} FIRST in Roundcube's contactlist_name_template (its default is '{name}'): under a
// template that shows other text of an entry before the name, or has no {name} at all, entries of
// other domains carry no department and no telephone numbers - that text would stand in front of
// the marker, or be shown without one (diag: WARN). A field such an entry does not carry is no search
// or sort criterion for it either (first and last name count only for an entry without a display
// name, whose shown name they are).
//
// Independently of this option every name from the directory is shown as plain text: besides
// control characters and \ " < > the plugin neutralises the notations that a later reader would
// decode - '&' (an HTML character reference such as &#64; in Roundcube's recipient widget) becomes
// the full-width ampersand, "=?" / "?=" (the delimiters of an RFC 2047 encoded word, decoded by the
// mail clients of the recipients) are split, curly brackets (template placeholders) become
// full-width, and so does '$', while a run of '%' is split by blanks (Roundcube's suggestion list
// reads "$&", "$1" ... as replacement patterns and "##...%%" as highlighting). Empty bracket pairs
// are removed and a text never starts with a closing or ends with an opening bracket (Roundcube
// deletes such pairs from a name composed with a template, which would join the split text again),
// nor with '=', '?' or '%'. A name - also a department, a phone, a group or organization label -
// that looks like an address is cut at the '@'; an entry without a name is shown with the local part
// of its address, treated the same way. Addresses themselves follow the address rule (README 3.4):
// an entry outside it is not offered at all.
$config['galbridge_mark_foreign_domains'] = true;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LIMITS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Minimum search word length (one value for all books). No query runs unless at least one word
// reaches it (global book: EVERY word); shorter words only narrow as word prefixes, so "Jan K"
// works. At most 5 words are used. In compose and in the Redirect dialog the plugin raises
// Roundcube's autocomplete_min_length to this value, because Roundcube's JavaScript stops asking
// the server once a prefix returned nothing.
$config['galbridge_min_search_length'] = 2;         // characters (1 .. 10)
$config['galbridge_max_search_length'] = 128;       // characters (16 .. 255); longer input matches nothing

// Caps of search results. A longer result LIST is cut with the notice "Only the first N directory
// matches are shown. Refine your search.", and so are the pages of a search result (pages beyond
// the cap are empty); the EXPORT of such a result is refused (a download cannot show that notice).
$config['galbridge_max_results'] = 500;             // domain book (10 .. 5000)
$config['galbridge_global_max_results'] = 100;      // global book (10 .. 5000)

// Compose to selected contacts and export of selected contacts: more selected directory contacts
// than this are refused - never silently cut to a prefix (compose shows a message; a refused export
// downloads nothing). A selection is complete or refused: a selected contact that cannot be read
// (gone, not visible to the user, a malformed id) refuses it as a whole. The same number bounds the
// directory contacts one request reads one by one (copying them to a personal address book): the
// rest is refused with "At most N directory contacts can be used at once".
$config['galbridge_max_ids'] = 200;                 // 1 .. 1000

// Members of one group that can be expanded at once: compose to group, a group picked in
// autocomplete, group export. A larger group is refused with a message that suggests a mailing
// list - never silently cut. The effective cap is the smaller of this value and Roundcube's
// max_group_members (when that is greater than 0); diag shows it. Above 10000 one request reads,
// maps and holds that many entries (diag WARN): keep it lower, or give the PHP of the web server the
// memory_limit and the time for it.
$config['galbridge_max_group_members'] = 500;       // 1 .. 100000

$config['galbridge_max_groups'] = 50;               // groups per book (1 .. 500); above it the book shows no groups
$config['galbridge_max_export'] = 2000;             // entries of a whole-book vCard export (1 .. 100000); above it refused (diag WARN above 10000, see above)
// Upper bound of the TOTALS shown for lists (100 .. 1000000); at the bound users see "Only the
// first N directory entries can be browsed. Use the search to find the others.", and list pages
// beyond it are empty. It bounds counting only: what an export or a recipient set holds is decided
// by the limit of that operation (galbridge_max_export, galbridge_max_group_members,
// galbridge_max_ids), whatever the relation between the values.
// A list page has at most the larger of Roundcube's max_pagesize and addressbook_pagesize entries,
// and never more than 1000: a larger page size is a page of that bound with "The directory shows at
// most N entries per page." (diag WARN when max_pagesize is 0 or above 1000).
$config['galbridge_max_count'] = 10000;

// There is deliberately NO per-session request rate limiter (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, section 11): a
// counter in the Roundcube session is not atomic - parallel autocomplete requests all pass - and
// would turn read-only requests into session writers. Resource control is: the caps above, the
// minimum search length, the connect and statement timeouts, the connection limit of the database
// account (set by SQL/grants.*.sql for both engines, shown by diag; a refusal at that limit fails
// only the request, it is not memoised for other sessions) and - REQUIRED on a server that is
// reachable from the Internet - per-client limits in the reverse proxy: a request-rate limit and a
// concurrency limit per client address on EVERY request passed to Roundcube's PHP, the concurrency
// limit well below the role's connection limit (SECURITY.md, "Hardening checklist for
// administrators", with an nginx example). A limit keyed on _action=autocomplete in the query
// string alone is not enough: Roundcube also reads _action from the POST body and a cookie.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LOGGING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Log channel "galbridge", written through Roundcube's log driver (the file
// <log_dir>/galbridge.log - Roundcube appends log_file_ext, default '.log' - or syslog: iRedMail
// sets log_driver 'syslog' with facility LOG_MAIL). Normal logging holds reason codes
// (denied_source, invalid_id, group_too_large, role_too_broad, db_error with the SQLSTATE, ...),
// the numeric Roundcube user id, book ids and counts - no search terms, names, addresses or
// passwords. Reason codes triggered by request input or by directory rows are logged at most once
// per request (a loop of requests still writes one line per request: limit request rates in the
// reverse proxy); db_error is logged for every failed query. With per_user_logging Roundcube
// writes the log into the user's own directory below log_dir when it exists and drops the line
// otherwise: GAL Bridge then writes ERROR and WARN lines to Roundcube's errors log instead. Under
// syslog every galbridge line has the priority INFO: filter on the level word (ERROR, WARN).
// true adds only the database driver's error text, the messages of unexpected exceptions and
// schema hints. The plugin never logs search terms or addresses itself, but those texts are not
// filtered for personal data (a driver error may quote part of a statement with the inlined search
// values) - enable it only while troubleshooting (diag warns while it is on, also when the variable
// ROUNDCUBE_GALBRIDGE_DEBUG switches it on). Independently of this option
// the DATABASE server's own logs (slow/general query log, PostgreSQL log_min_error_statement or
// log_min_duration_statement) can contain search terms, because rcube_db inlines bound values.
// The PostgreSQL reference grants switch statement logging off for the role (so there is no
// statement audit trail of it; an audit extension such as pgaudit or auto_explain has settings of
// its own, and diag warns while one would log the role's statements); MariaDB/MySQL have no such
// per-account setting - keep the general query log off or restrict who reads it. diag shows what
// applies to your server (SECURITY.md, "Logs").
$config['galbridge_debug'] = false;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RECOMMENDED ROUNDCUBE CORE SETTINGS (comments only)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Set these in Roundcube's own config/config.inc.php (iRedMail:
// /opt/www/roundcubemail/config/config.inc.php), NOT in this file.
//
// Enable the plugin (iRedMail ships array('managesieve', 'password', 'zipdownload')):
//   $config['plugins'] = ['managesieve', 'password', 'zipdownload', 'galbridge'];
//
// Sort contacts by display name. iRedMail keeps Roundcube's default 'surname', but the open-source
// iRedAdmin fills only mailbox.name, and Roundcube re-sorts search results by this column in PHP:
// with 'surname' most directory entries end up sorted by e-mail address. diag warns about any
// other value. Users can change it in Settings > Preferences > Contacts unless it is locked:
//   $config['addressbook_sort_col'] = 'name';
//   $config['dont_override'] = ['addressbook_sort_col'];      // optional; merge with your list
// Never add galbridge_* options to dont_override: Roundcube then stores their current value in the
// preferences of every user who saves settings - for an option set through a ROUNDCUBE_GALBRIDGE_*
// variable that is the value itself, galbridge_db_dsn with the database password (diag: FAIL).
//
// Autocomplete. In compose and in the Redirect dialog the plugin raises autocomplete_min_length to
// galbridge_min_search_length and appends its enabled books to autocomplete_addressbooks by itself
// (also for the autocomplete requests themselves). Set them explicitly only to
// make the values visible or to fix the order of the sources (duplicates are removed):
//   $config['autocomplete_min_length'] = 2;                    // >= galbridge_min_search_length
//   $config['autocomplete_addressbooks'] = ['sql', 'galbridge_domain', 'galbridge_global'];
//   $config['autocomplete_max'] = 15;                          // entries in the popup (default 15)
//   $config['autocomplete_threads'] = 0;                       // 0 (default) = all sources in one
//                                                              // request; n > 0 = parallel requests,
//                                                              // count them in the proxy limits
//   $config['autocomplete_single'] = false;                    // true = one address per contact
//
// Group expansion: Roundcube's own limit (default 0 = none) also caps directory groups - the
// smaller of it and galbridge_max_group_members wins:
//   $config['max_group_members'] = 0;
//
// Matching mode of the domain book (the global book always uses prefix search):
//   $config['addressbook_search_mode'] = 0;                    // 0 partial (default), 1 strict, 2 prefix
//
// Do not run the old sql_global_addressbooks plugin at the same time, and remove its _sql_* options;
// diag reports leftovers (docs/MIGRATION.md).
